package Vixen.Shaders.Pipeline

import Vixen.Shaders.Core
import Vixen.Shaders.Shading
import Vixen.Shaders.Geometry
import Vixen.Shaders.Material
import Vixen.Shaders.IrradianceFields
import Vixen.Shaders.PunctualShadows
import Vixen.Shaders.VirtualShadows
import Vixen.Shaders.DistanceFields

// The forward shading pass: written once against `IMaterialSurface`, instantiated per material.
//
// This is what the whole composition mechanism exists for. The shader knows nothing about metalness,
// glossiness, normal maps or emission — it fills a `MaterialData` from whatever feature the material
// chose, and shades it. Adding a material feature does not touch this file.
//
// "Forward *plus*" names the light culling: a compute pass bins lights into clusters so a fragment
// iterates only the lights that reach it instead of all of them. Both halves now exist —
// `ClusterCulling.rvn` is the binning pass — and `[Permutation] UseClusteredLights` chooses which
// loop this shader has:
//
//   off  a uniform array of `MaxLights`, filled by the host. Right for a scene with a handful of
//        lights, where a dispatch and a 450 KB buffer cost more than iterating all of them.
//   on   the cluster list, read from the buffer the culling pass wrote.
//
// It is a permutation rather than a branch because the two need different *code*: off, the clustered
// variant's `positionVS` stream folds away entirely, and with it the interpolator every fragment
// would otherwise pay for. A runtime branch would keep both loops in every variant.
//
// ⚠ It does **not** fold the bindings. A binding is in the plan because it is declared, not because
// a variant reads it, so a shader compiled with clustering on still declares the per-object light
// list and one with `UseImageBasedLighting` off still declares the environment cube — and a host
// still has to bind them, because a set is written wholly or not at all. Eliminating bindings no
// variant reads is a compiler feature Raven does not have; this comment claimed it did.
//
// **The lighting lives in `ClusteredShading`, which this derives from.** The light loops, the shadow
// cascades and the ambient term were all here, and all of them read exactly three things a rasterizer
// hands a fragment — the world position, the view-space position and the object index — and nothing
// else about how the pixel was found. Passing those three down as a `ShadingPoint` is what lets a
// visibility-buffer resolve shade the same surfaces with the same lights: see
// `docs/plan/22-virtualized-geometry.md` phase 5, and `VisibilityResolve.rvn`.

/// The split pass's three answers, in attachment order — see `SplitOutputs`.
///
/// Declaration order is target order, `SurfaceCardRaster`'s rule. With the permutation off only
/// `color` is ever written, and a target nothing writes is not an output — so the default variant
/// still presents the single attachment every existing composition binds.
struct ForwardTargets {
    /// Direct light, emissive and specular ambient; the material's alpha rides in `a`.
    var color: float4

    /// Diffuse albedo, with the material's own occlusion in `a` — 1 where it has none.
    var albedo: float4

    /// The shading normal in world space, with perceptual roughness in `a`.
    var normal: float4
}

shader ForwardPlus : ClusteredShading {

    /// Whether the fragment splits its answer across three targets instead of summing one.
    ///
    /// **The ambient split — what screen-space GI application was blocked on.** A combined frame
    /// has no normals plane for an AO or reflection pass to read, no albedo for a screen irradiance
    /// plane to modulate, and no seam a combine could put the ambient term back through: once
    /// direct and ambient are one number, occlusion can no longer reach only the half it belongs
    /// to. On, the fragment writes `ForwardTargets` — and the AMBIENT term moves out of target 0:
    ///
    ///   0  direct light + emissive (+ specular ambient — see below), alpha carried
    ///   1  diffuse albedo, material occlusion in alpha
    ///   2  world normal, roughness in alpha
    ///
    /// **Only *diffuse* ambient moves — the environment, field and probe indirect diffuse that
    /// `!AmbientCombine` rebuilds as albedo × irradiance × occlusion. Specular ambient (the
    /// prefiltered environment and the reflection probes) stays in target 0**, because no screen
    /// plane carries the directional lobe it needs; a reflections composite replacing it is a
    /// later refinement. See `ClusteredShading.AmbientSpecular`.
    ///
    /// A permutation rather than a branch because the two variants have different *interfaces*:
    /// off, the two extra members are never stored, and a target nothing writes is not an output —
    /// so the pipeline this compiles to still matches every one-attachment pass that exists.
    [Permutation] val SplitOutputs: bool = false

    /// What the surface does with a light, chosen per material.
    ///
    /// The second half of the same idea: `surface` decides what the point *is*, this decides how it
    /// answers a light. Two slots rather than one because the two vary independently — a clear coat
    /// over a metal-roughness base and over a specular-glossiness one is the same lobe — and folding
    /// them together would be one shader per pair.
    ///
    /// Declared here rather than on `ClusteredShading` because a composed model's own uniforms attach to
    /// the shader that declares the slot, and a host looks for them on the pass — see
    /// `ClusteredShading.Shade`, which this overrides to reach it.
    compose val shading: IShadingModel

    /// Where indirect diffuse comes from, when it comes from a field.
    compose val irradiance: IIrradianceSource = NoIrradiance

    /// Where ambient occlusion comes from, when it comes from a field.
    ///
    /// **The same clipmap `DistanceFieldAo` marches, marched here instead — and that is the whole
    /// idea.** A screen-space pass has to run after the depth and normals it reads, which in a
    /// forward frame means after the pass that shades; so its answer arrives one whole pass too late
    /// to reach the term it belongs to. Occlusion multiplies *indirect* light and nothing else —
    /// pre-multiplying it into direct light is the mistake that makes a scene look dirty rather than
    /// grounded — and a forward pass that has already summed direct and ambient into one number
    /// cannot take it back out.
    ///
    /// Marching here costs a walk per shaded pixel and needs no prepass, no second colour target, no
    /// screen-space buffer and no pass reordering. It also sees the whole world at the field's own
    /// resolution rather than only what is on screen, so a wall behind the camera darkens the floor
    /// in front of it — which is the thing screen-space occlusion structurally cannot do.
    ///
    /// `NoDistanceField` answers "nothing is near", so a project with no clipmap gets an unoccluded
    /// ambient term and no cost at all.
    compose val distanceField: IDistanceFieldSource = NoDistanceField

    /// Whether to march that field for occlusion.
    ///
    /// Off, and off is the picture this pass already drew. A permutation rather than a branch, so a
    /// frame that does not want it does not compile the march — which matters more here than it
    /// usually does, because the march is the most expensive thing in this shader.
    ///
    /// ⚠ It is not enough on its own, and neither is the composition on its own. Turning this on
    /// against a `NoDistanceField` slot marches nothing and costs nothing; naming the slot without
    /// this compiles the field's bindings into set 0 and never reads them. Both, plus
    /// `GlobalDistanceFieldRenderer.Passes` naming this pass so those bindings are actually filled.
    [Permutation] val UseDistanceFieldOcclusion: bool = false

    /// The composed model, reached from the shared light loops.
    override func Shade(d: MaterialData, a: ShadingAngles, s: ShadingVectors, alpha: float): float3 {
        return shading.Shade(d, a, s, alpha)
    }

    /// Where a spot or a point light's shadow comes from, when the frame renders one.
    ///
    /// **A slot and not a permutation, and one gate rather than two.** The occlusion march above needs
    /// both because a bound clipmap is useful for several things and marching it is the expensive
    /// half; a bound shadow atlas is useful for exactly this. `NoPunctualShadows` declares no bindings
    /// and compiles to `1f`, so a project that renders no atlas pays nothing and looks the way it did
    /// — and a project that names the atlas here has said everything it needs to say.
    ///
    /// ⚠ The other half is `PunctualShadowRenderer.Passes` naming this pass, so the three bindings the
    /// slot brings into set 0 are actually filled. Naming the slot and not the pass is a set written
    /// short, which is every draw refused rather than a shadow that is missing.
    compose val punctualShadow: IPunctualShadowSource = NoPunctualShadows

    /// Where the sun's shadow comes from, on exactly those terms.
    ///
    /// `NoDirectionalShadows` is the cascades: it answers `found = 0` for every point, and
    /// `ClusteredShading.Shadow` falls through to the atlas it already had. A project that composes
    /// `VirtualShadowLookup` gets phase 7's map where its pages are drawn and the cascades everywhere
    /// else, which is what lets the feature be turned on without a frame changing anywhere it has
    /// nothing to say.
    compose val directionalShadow: IDirectionalShadowSource = NoDirectionalShadows

    /// The composed map, reached from the shared shadow term.
    override func DirectionalShadow(positionWS: float3, n: float3, NdotL: float, viewDepth: float): DirectionalShadowSample {
        return directionalShadow.Sample(positionWS, n, NdotL, viewDepth)
    }

    /// The composed field, on the same terms.
    override func SampleIrradiance(position: float3, n: float3, v: float3): IrradianceSample {
        return irradiance.Sample(position, n, v)
    }

    /// The composed atlas, on the same terms.
    override func PunctualVisibility(light: PunctualLight, positionWS: float3, n: float3, NdotL: float): float {
        return punctualShadow.Visibility(light, positionWS, n, NdotL)
    }
    /// The material's surface features, chosen per material.
    compose val surface: IMaterialSurface


    /// Whether this pass's materials are records of one buffer rather than a set bound per draw.
    ///
    /// What `docs/bindless-materials.md` is about: a draw that binds a descriptor set per material
    /// cannot be merged with a draw that binds a different one. On it, the whole per-material block
    /// below becomes one record of a buffer subscripted by `materialIndex`.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Off by default, and the default is the half that runs everywhere. Records need
    /// `HasBindless`, which GL, WebGL2 and MoltenVK below argument-buffer tier 2 do not have — so a
    /// host asks for this only where the device said yes, and gets the pass it always had otherwise.
    [Permutation] val UseMaterialRecords: bool = false


    /// Whether the object's world matrix comes from a buffer instead of from a push constant.
    ///
    /// The other half of the same argument. Bindless materials stop a draw needing a descriptor set of
    /// its own; this stops it needing a *push* of its own — and a push is per draw by definition, so a
    /// pass that keeps one can never merge two draws however little else they differ in. On it, the
    /// matrix is at the object's slot in `transforms` and the draw carries the slot in its own
    /// `firstInstance`, which the API adds into `SV_InstanceID` before this shader runs.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Off by default, and off is not degraded. A pushed `mat4` costs no memory read at all, so
    /// this is a straight loss on any frame that is not merging — which is every frame on a device
    /// without `HasDrawIndirectCount`, since without a device-side draw count there is no compacted
    /// list to merge over. `TransformRenderFeature.EnableRecords` gates on exactly that.
    [Permutation] val UseTransformRecords: bool = false


    // --- Set 3: what varies per object ---------------------------------------
    //
    // Everything below used to be unmarked, which is set 2 — the material's. That was wrong in a way
    // that showed up as nothing: `ForwardLightingRenderFeature` writes exactly this block and binds
    // it at set 3, so the shader and the feature that fills it disagreed about which set it was in,
    // and neither said so. A set marker per binding is what makes the four-set convention a thing
    // the shader states rather than a thing a host remembers.

    /// Object to world, pushed rather than bound. Unused when `UseTransformRecords` is on.
    ///
    /// A push constant because that is what `TransformRenderFeature` already does with it, and the
    /// shader saying anything else would be the same silent disagreement this whole audit is about.
    /// It also leaves the per-draw block with exactly one owner — the lighting feature — where a
    /// block holding both a transform and a light list needs two features to agree on its layout.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Still declared with the records on, and the range is still in the pipeline layout. A binding
    /// is in the plan because it is declared and not because a variant reads it — the note at the top
    /// of this file — and a push constant is no different. Nothing pushes it in that variant, which
    /// costs the layout 64 of its guaranteed 128 bytes and costs a frame nothing.
    ///
    /// `worldViewProjection` is gone with it: it was world × the view's matrix, computed per object
    /// on the CPU and uploaded per object, where the vertex stage can multiply two matrices it
    /// already has. Sixty-four bytes less per object, and one less thing that can be a frame out of
    /// date with the camera it was built from.
    [PushConstant] var world: mat4


    /// Which record of the material buffer this draw's material is, when materials are records.
    ///
    /// **A push constant, and it was a per-object block member — which was the wrong shape.** A
    /// variant is keyed `(material, flags, shader)`, so one variant is one material; a batch is keyed
    /// on the variant, so every object in a merged draw has the *same* material and the same record.
    /// It is per *draw* and never per object, and putting it in the per-object block meant a
    /// clustered frame — which binds no per-draw set at all, because a fragment finds its own lights
    /// in the grid — never delivered it. Bindless materials and clustered lighting were quietly
    /// exclusive.
    ///
    /// It also takes a cross-feature write with it: the lighting feature used to write the material
    /// feature's number into its own block, which its own comment apologised for. And the block is 68
    /// bytes against Vulkan's guaranteed 128, so it fits everywhere `world` already did.
    ///
    /// ⚠ `[MaterialIndex("UseMaterialRecords")]` rather than `[MaterialIndex]`: the marker turns this
    /// shader's whole per-material block into one record of a buffer, and that is what a device with
    /// bindless wants and not what GL, WebGL2 or MoltenVK below argument-buffer tier 2 can do
    /// (ADR-011). Conditional on the permutation, one shader is both — and the *default is the
    /// fallback*, so a host that never asked gets exactly the pass it had.
    [PushConstant] [MaterialIndex("UseMaterialRecords")] var materialIndex: uint = 0u


    // --- Set 1: what varies per view -----------------------------------------
    //
    // The block every shader in the frame shares, and the reason it is stated here in the same terms
    // `ViewConstants` writes it: a descriptor set survives a pipeline change only if the layouts
    // agree up to that set, so what set 1 holds is a contract between shaders rather than any one
    // shader's business. Only a block belongs here — a texture or a buffer would make two shaders'
    // set 1 incompatible and the shared set unbindable.

    /// World to clip for this view.
    [PerView] var viewProjection: mat4


    // --- Set 0: the scene ----------------------------------------------------

    /// Every object's world matrix, at the object's own slot. Read when `UseTransformRecords` is on.
    ///
    /// Per *frame* and not per view, because where an object is does not depend on who is looking —
    /// which is what lets the shadow pass and the camera pass read one buffer, and what would make a
    /// per-view copy of it so much waste.
    [PerFrame] var transforms: Buffer<ObjectTransform>


    /// Which record this frame's objects start at, added to the instance index.
    ///
    /// **An index rather than a bound offset, and the reason is the host's.** The buffer holds one
    /// region per frame in flight — a descriptor bound at zero reads the region another frame is
    /// writing — and the route a resource takes to a set carries a handle a host named and has
    /// nowhere to put an offset. So the whole ring is bound and this says where this frame begins.
    /// `UploadBuffer` recommends the same thing for everything else it holds.
    ///
    /// Declared here rather than at the top of the block for the same reason `materialIndex` sits
    /// where it does: std140 leaves four bytes after a `float3`, so put here it costs the block
    /// nothing and put first it would cost sixteen.
    [PerFrame] var transformBase: int = 0


    stream var positionWS: float3

    stream var normalWS: float3

    stream var tangentWS: float4

    stream var uv: float2


    /// Which object this fragment belongs to, when the scalars are records.
    ///
    /// The instance index, carried across the rasteriser so the fragment stage can address the same
    /// record the vertex stage could. `SV_InstanceID` is a vertex-stage built-in and there is no
    /// fragment counterpart, so a varying is the only route — and an integer varying is `flat` by
    /// necessity rather than by choice: there is no interpolation an index could take.
    ///
    /// Costs a location only in the variant that reads it, because the write below is inside the
    /// permutation and a stream nothing writes is not an output.
    stream var objectIndex: int


    /// View-space position, for finding the fragment's cluster.
    ///
    /// Carried from the vertex stage rather than recovered in the fragment stage, because Raven has no
    /// fragment-stage position built-in — there is no `SV_Position` to read on the way in, only to
    /// write on the way out. Interpolating it costs a location, which the unclustered variant does
    /// not pay: the write below is inside the permutation, so with clustering off there is no write,
    /// and a stream nothing writes is not an output.
    stream var positionVS: float3


    /// The vertex stage.
    ///
    /// `SV_InstanceID` is taken whether or not the records are on, and taking it costs nothing: it is
    /// a built-in the stage is handed, not an input anybody has to bind. Reading it *conditionally*
    /// would be the wrong shape — a parameter cannot appear inside a permutation — and this way the
    /// two variants differ only in where the matrix comes from.
    [VertexShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Position")]
    func Vertex(
        position: float3,
        normal: float3,
        tangent: float4,
        texcoord: float2,
        [Semantic("SV_InstanceID")] instance: int
    ): float4 {
        // Whichever way the host sends it. The permutation folds, so the variant that pushes has no
        // buffer read and the variant that reads has no push — rather than a branch every vertex takes
        // on a value that is the same for the whole frame.
        var objectToWorld = world

        if (UseTransformRecords) {
            objectToWorld = transforms[transformBase + instance].world
        }

        if (UseObjectRecords) {
            objectIndex = instance
        }

        positionWS = Math.TransformPoint(objectToWorld, position)
        normalWS = Math.TransformNormalRigid(objectToWorld, normal)
        tangentWS = float4(Math.TransformDirection(objectToWorld, tangent.xyz), tangent.w)
        uv = texcoord

        if (UseClusteredLights) {
            positionVS = Math.TransformPoint(view, positionWS)
        }

        // Two matrices the stage already has, rather than their product uploaded per object.
        return Transform.ObjectToClip(viewProjection, positionWS)
    }


    [FragmentShader]
    func Fragment(): ForwardTargets {
        // The material fills the surface. Every feature reads what the previous one left, which is
        // what `inout` buys: adding a feature to the chain changes no other feature's signature.
        var d: MaterialData
        MaterialDefaults.Begin(d, normalWS, tangentWS, uv)
        surface.Compute(d)

        val v = Math.SafeNormalize(viewPosition - positionWS)
        val n = Normals.FaceForward(Math.SafeNormalize(d.normalWS), v)
        val alpha = Brdf.Alpha(d.perceptualRoughness)

        // The three things lighting needs to know about where this pixel is, which used to be read from
        // the streams inside each function. A resolve fills the same struct from a triangle.
        //
        // ⚠ The two optional streams are read **inside their own permutations**, matching the guards the
        // vertex stage writes them under. A ternary would read both operands and so declare the input in
        // every variant — and a fragment input the vertex stage does not write is a pipeline the driver
        // refuses, which is what this looked like before the guards went back in.
        var p: ShadingPoint
        p.positionWS = positionWS
        p.positionVS = float3(0f, 0f, 0f)
        p.objectIndex = -1

        if (UseClusteredLights) {
            p.positionVS = positionVS
        }

        if (UseObjectRecords) {
            p.objectIndex = objectIndex
        }

        // ⚠ **Into `d.occlusion`, which is read by the ambient term and by nothing else.** That is the
        // whole reason this is here rather than multiplied over the frame afterwards: `Ambient` hands
        // it to `Ibl.Diffuse` and `Ibl.Specular`, and `Direct` and `Punctual` never see it. A lamp
        // three metres away still lights the inside of a corner; the sky no longer does.
        //
        // Before `Direct` for readability only — occlusion cannot reach either of the two lines below.
        //
        // ⚠ **The radius is a room rather than a crease, and that is what makes this useful indoors.**
        // A screen-space kernel measures half a metre and finds the join between a crate and the
        // floor; five metres finds the *room* — so standing inside a closed box, most of the
        // hemisphere is wall and the sky's contribution falls away, which is the thing a shadow map
        // cannot do because the sky is not a shadow caster. The cost is that a wall darkens the floor
        // for five metres out rather than for one, which is what ambient occlusion at a room scale
        // looks like and is not a bug.
        //
        // Seven samples up the normal, against `DistanceFieldAoRenderer`'s five: the radius grew, so
        // the step between samples would otherwise have grown with it and started stepping over
        // whole crates.
        if (UseDistanceFieldOcclusion) {
            d.occlusion = d.occlusion * distanceField.OcclusionField(positionWS, n, 5f, 7, 1f)
        }

        var color = Direct(d, p, n, v, alpha)
        color += Punctual(d, p, n, v, alpha)

        var targets: ForwardTargets

        if (SplitOutputs) {
            // Diffuse ambient is the combine's job now; the specular half has no plane to move to
            // and stays — see the permutation's own note and `AmbientSpecular`.
            if (UseImageBasedLighting) {
                color += AmbientSpecular(d, p, n, v)
            }

            targets.color = float4(color + d.emissive, d.alpha)

            // `d.occlusion` is the material's own term times the field march above, when that ran —
            // exactly the occlusion the ambient term would have seen here, handed to the combine
            // to multiply into the ambient it rebuilds.
            targets.albedo = float4(d.diffuseColor, d.occlusion)
            targets.normal = float4(n, d.perceptualRoughness)
        } else {
            // Either source of ambient light brings the whole term in, and the term itself decides
            // which halves it has. Gating on the IBL flag alone would make a project that has a
            // field and no environment cube get no ambient at all.
            if (UseImageBasedLighting || UseIrradianceField) {
                color += Ambient(d, p, n, v)
            }

            targets.color = float4(color + d.emissive, d.alpha)
        }

        return targets
    }
}
